**约定 75.1（complete word and costs）。** A complete word contains every source-dependent bit: numerical fields, precision, branch, scale, selection, program if source-dependent, identity, required certificate, and required evidence. One fixed public interpreter is used for all sources. Its finite mathematical grammar, selection rules, installation and evaluation are specified in §§76,79–80,83; none depends on an unrecorded source-specific table. The fixed mode header is charged. For finite-cap lower bounds, changing a source-dependent program changes the word. There are at most 2^(B+1)-1 binary words of length at most B, including the empty word.

Define C_N(E) as the minimum possible worst-case complete word length on the actual parameter domain with at most N leaves, for a fixed universal interpreter and source-aware selection, with no mandatory external evidence beyond the numerical/selection fields. This definition concerns representation, not what the original observer can acquire. If the interface mandates evidence of length M(s), it is part of the full word and the upper below becomes its numerical core plus M(s)+self-delimiting framing. Such M is not assumed bounded; the numerical law is not asserted for a word that must also retain arbitrary source identities or full certificates.

The following interfaces are different. (a) Original finite replies: exactly the original three reports and no later names. Their promised error and finite representation are explicit. (b) Source-aware set-theoretic selection: knowledge of the actual source proves existence of a word, without claiming an observer can compute it. (c) A paid historical-name archive: before closure it returns approximations of the SAME three historical values at requested precisions. This is extra access to an archive, not another source Read or a consequence of Stop. (d) Truthful finite source/size/gap/neutral/zero certificates: truth is a supplier premise, not a consequence of recognizing integer syntax. (e) Exact finite rational presentations: numerator/denominator strings, with separately charged production. (f) Arbitrary-real registers or infinite names: these are not finite words.

